WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
Speaker 1: Why don't we go next to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:08.960
Diego from California.
Speaker 2: Hello. Hello. What's going on? I had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:17.840
question about what do you think about the&nbsp;
growth of people being interested in socialism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.440
or any sort of radical kind of&nbsp;
socialist ideologies in America?&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:32.400
Speaker 1: I don't know. I mean, I think it's not&nbsp;
surprising that when a system is failing that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.880
creates a window of opportunity of getting people&nbsp;
to jump on board with really different systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:45.840
So like, for example, I would agree with the&nbsp;
Socialists that our form of capitalism has failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:53.120
so many people. But for me, the solution is well&nbsp;
regulated capitalism called social democracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.880
in the northern European model. But I understand&nbsp;
how if someone is just getting introduced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.240
some of this stuff, whatever it seems the most&nbsp;
different from what we have, we'll have a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.200
amount of appeal. So I guess I'd say it's not it's&nbsp;
not super surprising that that's what's going on.&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.760
Speaker 2: Yeah. With the Nordic model,&nbsp;
what I found interesting is the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:21.360
of like more radical socialist elements in those&nbsp;
countries that helped push for some of the social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.360
democratic things like rent controls and other&nbsp;
government programs. Do you think that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.720
be achieved here or what's your opinion on that?
Speaker 1: Generally, could which part be achieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.040
here? Just having rent controls, I guess.
Speaker 2: Do you think a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:43.120
revolutionary radical socialist element in the&nbsp;
country could serve to bolster social democracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.920
in America like it did at times in Scandinavia?
Speaker 1: I think so. And I've said before I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:56.351
an interview with a streamer who goes by the&nbsp;
name Varsh. Some people say Vash, some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.351 --> 00:02:02.320
say the oche. And while he is a socialist and I&nbsp;
am not, we were in agreement that right now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.960
Social Democrats and the Socialists should really&nbsp;
be pushing for very similar socially Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.520
policies, because if there is to be socialism,&nbsp;
it will quite likely go through social democracy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.240
Now, there's a different revolutionary perspective&nbsp;
which says, no, no, no, no, no, there's no need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.440
go through social democracy. You just got to crush&nbsp;
capitalism and then put in socialism. I just even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.720
though I'm not a socialist, to me, the most likely&nbsp;
path to socialism is through social democracy. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.880
yeah, I agree that right now Social Democrats&nbsp;
and actual socialists could very much be united&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:41.040
on getting to social Democratic policy if and when&nbsp;
we get the US to be more like that. At that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.720
the Social Democrats and the Socialists&nbsp;
can fight it out for whether we keep going.&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.520
Speaker 2: Yeah, I generally agree at this current&nbsp;
point with both of the parties being pretty like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.560
the Democrat and Republican parties being pretty&nbsp;
like pretty rough when it comes to capitalism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.200
they're kind of just unbridled support for it. So&nbsp;
it does seem like anyone left of that is kind of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.400
it's kind of left out in the dust, you know?
Speaker 1: Yeah, no, I don't. There's a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.840
of people that are just outside of that two&nbsp;
party paradigm. And it's not surprising that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.600
when something else comes along that's appeal to&nbsp;
them, that they would gravitate towards it. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.840
no shock at all.
Speaker 2: Mm hmm. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.680
thanks for answer my question. That's cool.
Speaker 1: My pleasure. All right, sir. Diego,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.320
very much appreciate the call, sir. Sure.

